// Deposit sizes (#439): seraphhorizons' SmallerDeposits switch (README.md "Ore cells").
// Read by Ore/Game/DepositSizes.cs, which scales each Interesting Ore Gen vein variant of a metal
// by that metal's volume factor (Ore/Core/VeinScaling.cs: disc veins by √factor on the radius,
// tube veins on their tendril count). A factor may be over 1: the vein grows past Interesting Ore
// Gen's own size.
//
// Basis: one ore a block, twice the ingots of before. Ore blocks drop one raw ore and no crystallised
// ore (the SingleOreDrops switch; Ore/Core/SingleOreDrops.cs), and deposits hold twice the metal they
// did at vanilla's 1.25 a block: every factor below is 2.5 times what it was (twice the metal at four
// fifths of the ore a block), every target and size tier twice, and each median is the figure
// measured at 1.25 a block re-expressed at one (x 0.8). The sizes are on this basis with the switch
// off too, so ore then gives a quarter more than they say. A world created before this keeps the old
// basis: its factors are 0.4 of these and never above 1, its targets and tiers half
// (Ore/Core/SingleOreDrops.cs, DepositBasis; the world's ore record, doubledDeposits).
//
// A metal's factor is targetIngots / medianIngots, or "factor" given outright:
// - medianIngots: the median deposit measured over 5 seeds x 26 km² of the pack before this change
//   (epic #435's table; a deposit is all ore of one metal within 150 m, an ore block 1.25 chunks), times 0.8.
// - targetIngots: the typical deposit, twice what the epic wanted for a group of about four: copper and
//   iron 800, tin, zinc, bismuth, lead and nickel 300, silver and gold 160, titanium, chromium and
//   platinum 300.
//   Its small / large tiers (about ÷2.7 and ×2.5) come from the veins' own spread, which is kept.
// - smallIngots, largeIngots: the bottom and top of the metal's range (the epic's small and large);
//   with targetIngots, the deposit registry's size tiers (#443): small, medium and large are the
//   bottom, middle and top third of smallIngots..largeIngots, and below a tenth of smallIngots a
//   deposit counts as worked out (Ore/Core/DepositRegistry.cs, DepositSizing).
// - Coal and the industrial minerals got a quarter cut at most (0.75): they were one deposit per
//   3–10 km² and are not what the walk is for. Their yield doubles with the metals' (1.875).
// Gold, silver (apart from the silver inside copper and lead veins), nickel, titanium and chromium
// come mostly from hydrothermal districts. District veins are sized to smallIngots / targetIngots /
// largeIngots directly (config/ore-districts.json, Ore/Core/DistrictVeins.cs), not by the factor.
// Argentiferous galena (galena_nativesilver) is a lead ore since #690, but the silver and lead medians
// were measured with it counted as silver, so silver's includes the galena subdeposits. Worldgen did
// not change (Ore/Core/OreMetals.cs, WorldgenMetalOf), so the factors stand; docs/oregen.md,
// "Argentiferous galena".
// These began as a first cut from volume ∝ radius² × thickness (discs) and count × length (tubes).
// Correction from the survey of #445 (docs/oregen.md): the anchored veins of copper, iron, bismuth,
// zinc and platinum came out at about twice the target (eight veins pooled over three seeds, median
// 2.1x; one or three veins per metal, so the correction is shared, not per metal). Those metals
// carry "factor" outright: half of target / median. Tin's one vein was small (0.3x, a deep
// cassiterite seam) and lead, nickel, silver, gold, titanium and chromium had no anchored vein in
// the survey; they keep target / median until a wider survey measures them.
// Retuned for #945 on the census of #946 (tools/ore-survey --census, 10 seeds x 640 m, stable veins and
// grade pockets on): the ore-cell medians were copper 165, iron 316, zinc 67, bismuth 111 and platinum
// 89 ingots, the stable veins having shrunk them to about 0.75x. Each of those factors was multiplied by
// target / that median; zinc, which grows less than its factor near the census's 50-ingot floor, was
// then fitted over three runs (0.0835: median 100, 0.189: 214, 0.129: 141). Tin (128) and lead (140)
// were within 25% of target and keep theirs. docs/oregen.md, "Ore cells after #945". (The figures in
// this history are on the old basis.)
{
  "copper":   { "medianIngots": 2853.6, "targetIngots": 800, "factor": 0.34, "smallIngots": 300, "largeIngots": 2000 },
  "iron":     { "medianIngots": 3844.8, "targetIngots": 800, "factor": 0.13175, "smallIngots": 300, "largeIngots": 2000 },
  "tin":      { "medianIngots": 716.8,  "targetIngots": 300, "smallIngots": 120, "largeIngots": 800 },
